  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nA clear pattern shift later this week is likely to lead to a\nquasi-blocking flow regime as the flow becoming highly amplified\nover the eastern Pacific through the entire CONUS and Canada. The\nridge over the Intermountain West is expected to extend into \nsouthern Canada by Thursday with deep troughing developing off the\nWest Coast and over the Northeastern U.S. Despite the highly \namplified flow, the upstream trough to our northwest should be \nenough to kick the ridge just to the east of our region later \nThursday into Friday as a modest shortwave trough peels off from \nthe parent large scale trough. Model guidance is still struggling \nto come to a full consensus on the track and the strength of the \nincoming disturbance for Friday into Saturday. Trends over the \npast couple of days have shifted the system more over Baja and \nSonora Mexico while also showing some noticeable weakening as it \ntracks by just to our south on Friday. A second shortwave trough \nmay also track by just to our north on Saturday.\n\nDespite the lingering model uncertainty later this week, we are\nnearly assured to have increasing higher level clouds early on \nFriday followed by some weak lower level moisture advection out of\nthe south during the latter half of Friday. Ensemble mean PWATs \nare shown to increase to between 175-200% of normal starting \nFriday, but due to the bulk of the system energy likely bypassing \nto the south on Friday and to the north on Saturday we are not \nanticipating widespread rainfall. NBM PoPs are still mostly \nbetween 10-25% for the Arizona lower deserts to 20-35% for the \nhigher terrain north and east of the Phoenix area from midday \nFriday through much of Saturday. Rainfall is expected to be light\nwith total amounts of a trace to at most 0.10\" across the lower \ndeserts to localized amounts of 0.10-0.20\" over the high terrain. \nWe also can't completely rule out a weak thunderstorm or two on \nFriday focused across far southern Arizona since forecast MUCAPE \ndoes increase to 200-300 J/kg. Temperatures will start to drop \non Friday with lower desert highs falling back into the upper 70s\nto around 80 degrees followed by mid to upper 70s over the \nweekend. The unsettled weather pattern is expected to continue \ninto next week with even better rain chances by around next \nTuesday.",
